# Findings the gitleaks history scan reports that are not secrets.
#
# Each entry is <commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line> and must name a specific commit,
# so nothing here can silence a future leak — a real one would arrive in a new
# commit with a different fingerprint. Keep it that way: no wildcards, and one
# comment per entry saying why.
#
# --- 1 -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# api/contract_test.go declared an invented API key as a single
# `cg_<id>_<secret>` literal, which a scanner cannot tell apart from a real
# leaked one. It never authenticated anything: the only thing that accepts it is
# a stub in the same file, and the value is compared verbatim.
#
# The line has since been rewritten to assemble the key from named parts, so the
# working tree is clean and no new commit reproduces this. Only the historical
# commit still holds the old text, and this entry covers exactly that commit.
#
# When this entry can go — and it is not "after the merge", as an earlier
# version of this comment claimed. PR #56 was squash-merged, so the commit is no
# longer an ancestor of develop, and the scan still finds it: `gitleaks git`
# walks every ref, and `feature/e3-s5-openapi-docs` still exists on origin.
#
# Verified by cloning: with all branches present the finding returns, with
# `--single-branch --branch develop` it does not. So the entry stays until that
# merged branch is deleted on origin, and can be dropped in the same change.
